===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|weke}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wiċe}}, {{m|ang|wucu|t=week}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wikā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wikǭ|t=turn, succession, change, week}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*weyg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*weyk-|t=to bend, wind, turn, yield}}. Related to {{cog|gem-pro|*wīkaną|t=to bend, yield, cease}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|Wiek}}, {{cog|fy|wike}}, {{cog|nl|week}}, {{cog|de|Woche}}, {{cog|da|uge}}, {{cog|nn|veke}}, {{cog|sv|vecka}}, {{cog|is|vika}}, {{cog|got|𐍅𐌹𐌺𐍉|t=turn for temple service}}, {{cog|la|vicis}}, {{cog|fi|viikko}}. Related also to {{cog|ang|wīcan|t=to yield, give way}}, English {{doublet|en|weak|wick|notext=1}}.

